//! # State machines
//!
//! A machine declares a closed set of events, states, and transition rules.
//! `step` mutates a machine value, and `state_name` returns its current state.
//! Every state/event cell must be covered. `default { state }` is the explicit
//! no-op stay rule for all cells not otherwise listed.
//!
//! ```hew
//! machine Counter {
//!     events { Inc; Reset; }
//!     state Zero;
//!     state NonZero { value: i64; }
//!     on Inc: Zero => NonZero { value: 1 }
//!     on Inc: NonZero => NonZero reenter { value: state.value + 1 }
//!     on Reset: NonZero => Zero;
//!     default { state }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     var counter: Counter = .Zero;
//!     let _ = counter.step(.Inc).expect("machine step failed");
//!     let _ = counter.step(.Inc).expect("machine step failed");
//!     println(counter.state_name());
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Events, outputs, guards, and hooks
//!
//! `events` declares typed inputs. An optional `emits` block declares typed
//! output values collected by each step. A `when` guard refines a transition;
//! guarded rules need an unconditional fallback because a guard alone does not
//! establish exhaustiveness. State `entry` and `exit` hooks run synchronously
//! and may emit outputs.
//!
//! ```hew
//! machine Meter {
//!     events { Reading { value: i64; } }
//!     emits { Alarm { value: i64; } }
//!
//!     state Watching {
//!         peak: i64;
//!         entry { emit Alarm { value: state.peak }; }
//!     }
//!
//!     on Reading: Watching => Watching reenter
//!         when event.value > state.peak {
//!         emit Alarm { value: event.value };
//!         Watching { peak: event.value }
//!     }
//!     on Reading: Watching => Watching { state }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     var meter: Meter = .Watching { peak: 0 };
//!     let report = meter.step(.Reading { value: 7 })
//!         .expect("machine step failed");
//!     for output in report.outputs {
//!         match output {
//!             .Alarm { value } => println(value),
//!         }
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Evaluation order for a changing fixed target is exit hook, transition body,
//! then entry hook. A same-state target runs only its body unless marked
//! `reenter`. A computed `_` target runs exit and entry because its resulting
//! state is known only after the body evaluates. Hooks and rules are pure:
//! they cannot perform I/O, spawn, suspend, or call an unproved indirect
//! function.
//!
//! A rule head names the state the transition produces, so the body carries
//! only what the head cannot: nothing for a unit target, the field list for a
//! fielded one, and an expression when the value is computed. `state.field`
//! reads the source state and `event.field` reads the event payload; `self` is
//! an actor or method receiver and is not bound here.
//!
//! `reenter` marks a self-state transition that re-enters the state's
//! lifecycle rather than being treated as an ordinary stay. It is useful when
//! a self-transition must rebuild state or trigger state-entry behaviour.
//!
//! Wildcards are asymmetric: `_` is allowed for a state pattern, but events
//! must be named explicitly. For example, `on Kill: _ => Dead,` covers Kill
//! from every state, but there is no wildcard-event spelling. Explicit cells
//! win over wildcard-state rules. Omitting a cell without a matching default
//! is a compile error, so state and event exhaustiveness is enforced rather
//! than inferred at runtime.
//!
//! ## Generated API
//!
//! | Generated item | Meaning |
//! | --- | --- |
//! | `Name` | State enum, including each leaf configuration |
//! | `Name.Event` | Input variants declared in `events` |
//! | `NameOutput` | Output variants declared in `emits` |
//! | `NameStepDisposition` | `Taken` for an explicit rule or `Ignored` for `default` |
//! | `NameStep` | Must-use report with `outputs`, `disposition`, input tag, and executed rule ordinals |
//! | `value.step(event, limit: 1024)` | Mutates the value on success and returns `Result<NameStep, MachineStepError>` |
//! | `value.state_name()` | Returns the active state tag as a string |
//! | `NameScope` and `NameSubtree` | Compound-state selectors and typed departure snapshots |
//! | `value.step_observed_with_limit(...)` | Steps while capturing requested compound departures |
//!
//! A failed step leaves the original value unchanged. `emit` only appends to
//! the report; it does not feed another event into the machine or perform the
//! action represented by the output. The report's `input` is the external
//! event tag, and `rules` lists zero-based source rule ordinals in execution
//! order. Every machine implements `Machine`; its associated `Event`, `Step`,
//! `Scope`, and `Subtree` types let generic code use the same evaluator.
//!
//! ## Const parameters and composite states
//!
//! A machine can declare `usize` const parameters with defaults. Their values
//! are visible in guards, bodies, and hooks. Nested `state` blocks form
//! composite states; exactly one direct child is `initial`, and targeting the
//! composite descends through initial children to a leaf.
//!
//! ```hew
//! machine Session {
//!     events { Open; Authenticate; Close; }
//!     state Closed;
//!     state Live {
//!         initial state Authenticating;
//!         state Active;
//!         on Authenticate: Authenticating => Active;
//!         on Close: _ => Closed;
//!     }
//!     on Open: Closed => Live;
//!     default { state }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ```hew
//! machine Retry<const MAX: usize = 3> {
//!     events { Fail; }
//!     state Trying { attempts: usize; }
//!     state Exhausted;
//!     on Fail: Trying => Trying when state.attempts + 1 < MAX {
//!         attempts: state.attempts + 1
//!     }
//!     on Fail: Trying => Exhausted;
//!     on Fail: Exhausted => Exhausted reenter;
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Leaf rules have priority over rules on their nearest composite ancestor,
//! then broader ancestors, machine-wide wildcard rules, and finally `default`.
//! Named sibling transitions keep their nearest common ancestor active; a
//! computed `_` target exits and re-enters the full active branch.
//!
//! ## Parallel states and stabilization
//!
//! `parallel state` contains named `region` blocks and stores one active child
//! per region. One external input can take compatible transitions in several
//! regions; their rule ordinals appear together in the step report. A
//! conflicting deeper transition wins over an ancestor transition.
//!
//! `final state` completes its enclosing compound. Use
//! `on completion: Compound => Target` to react to completion and
//! `always: Source => Target` for an eventless rule. After handling the input,
//! `step` repeatedly selects completion rules and then `always` rules until the
//! machine is stable. Each nonempty transition set consumes one unit of the
//! step limit; invalid or exhausted limits return `MachineStepError` without
//! committing partial state, outputs, or history.
//!
//! ## History, observation, and wire values
//!
//! `Target history` restores the last immediate child configuration of a
//! compound, falling back to its initial child when nothing has been saved.
//! History belongs to the machine value, so copies evolve independently.
//! [std.machine.history](std.machine.history.html) retains complete requested
//! subtrees and restores them explicitly; the generated `NameScope`,
//! `NameSubtree`, and observation step API are its low-level surface.
//!
//! [std.machine.watch](std.machine.watch.html) gives one owner exclusive step
//! access and lets bounded subscribers observe committed state revisions. A
//! lagging subscriber gets a typed lag error instead of silently skipping
//! revisions. Outputs remain with the caller of `step`.
//!
//! `#[wire] machine Name` applies the normal wire schema rules to generated
//! states, events, outputs, reports, regions, history, scopes, and snapshots.
//! Use `hew wire check` before publishing a schema change; see
//! [wire data](language.wire.html).
//!
//! `hew machine diagram file.hew` validates a machine and renders Mermaid.
//! Add `--format graphviz` or `--format json`, and use `--machine Name` when a
//! file contains several declarations.
